repository-code: "https://github.com/queelius/trapdoor-computing"
url: "https://metafunctor.com"
abstract: >-
  We propose the cipher map as a unifying framework for
  parameterized-leakage outsourced function evaluation in the
  quantitative information flow tradition. A cipher map is a total
  function on bit strings whose privacy properties emerge from a one-way
  trapdoor combined with a frequency-equalized output distribution; its
  value-side security is characterized by four measurable properties
  (totality, representation uniformity, correctness, composability). The
  acceptance-predicate apparatus exposes a Pareto frontier between
  expected codeword length and total-variation leakage, refining the
  Shannon-frequency duality. A multi-instance composition theorem gives a
  closed form for shared-function leakage, with randomized encoding as
  the deployment-side defense. The framework subsumes Bloom filters,
  frequency-hiding encryption, and encrypted Boolean search as instances.
keywords:
  - trapdoor computing
  - cipher maps
  - quantitative information flow
  - parameterized leakage
  - acceptance predicate
  - Shannon-frequency duality
  - Le Cam bound
  - homophonic substitution
  - encrypted search
  - perfect hash function
  - privacy-preserving computation
